WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:05.560
The second season of DC Universe's Titans
has given hardcore comics fans plenty of hidden

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.500
things to find.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.220
There are meaningful passcodes, direct swipes
from comic book covers, and mentions of unseen

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.400
beloved characters.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.360
We're breaking down all this and more in our
round-up of Easter eggs you might have missed

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.880
in Titans Season 2.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.020
The gun Joe Chill used to murder Bruce Wayne's
parents makes something of an appearance in

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.660
the Titans Season 2 premiere.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.280
Trigon subjects the Titans to the same kinds
of visions he used on Dick Grayson in the

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.180
Season 1 finale.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.640
In the case of Jason Todd, he inserts the
new Robin into the end of Dick's vision, just

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.220
after Dick has killed Batman.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.140
Dick tells Jason that the younger hero is
an animal who "needs to be put down."

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.840
Their fight ends with the infamous pistol
sliding from its broken case to the floor

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.740
near Jason.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.400
When Jason grabs the gun, Dick suggests Bruce
would never keep the gun loaded.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.200
"No.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.760
But I do."

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.880
Does the Titans version of the caped crusader
really keep Joe Chill's gun in the Batcave,

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.520
like the Batman of Justice League?

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.160
Does Jason actually keep Chill's gun loaded
while he's living with Bruce?

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.300
Or is all this just part of the illusion?

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.100
Considering we know that in the comics Jason
Todd eventually goes down some dark roads

00:01:09.109 --> 00:01:13.029
— including becoming the lethal vigilante
the Red Hood — it's possible that what he

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:15.400
says isn't just part of Trigon's nightmare.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.200
If not, it would not only be a betrayal of
Bruce Wayne's trust, but would point to a

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.820
dark turn for the character in the show equal
to what happens in the comics.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.079
Just before Rachel defeats Trigon in the Titans
Season 2 premiere, the possessed Titans wait

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.060
nearby, with the dark splotching around their
eyes signaling their new loyalties.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.060
One shot in particular mirrors the cover of
a comic that marked one of the more memorable

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.240
battles between the Titans and Trigon.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.580
The cover to 1985's New Teen Titans #4 shows
the heroes standing in a row with their altered

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.080
eyes letting us know they're under Trigon's
control.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.280
The players aren't all the same — the cover
to the comic shows Beast Boy, Kid Flash, and

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.080
Cyborg.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.820
Also, their eyes are colored red rather than
the skin around their eyes taking on that

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:55.960
inky black look.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.480
Regardless, the meaning behind the respective
eye effects are clear, as is the inspiration

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.880
the show takes from the comic.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.080
In New Teen Titans #4, the heroes are forced
to individually wrestle with Trigon's power

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.260
just as they are in the Season 2 premiere,
but in a much more literal sense.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:13.659
Rather than being exposed to nightmare visions
that usually lead the heroes to making horrible

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:18.269
— and often lethal — choices, the heroes
of New Teen Titans #4 fight gray-skinned,

00:02:18.269 --> 00:02:21.200
red-eye shadow versions of themselves in the
real world.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.879
One of the more interesting moments in the
brief scenes involving Slade Wilson — aka

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.020
Deathstroke — in Season 2 so far is when
he enters a passcode to open his private arsenal.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.100
The conversation between Slade and Wintergreen
suggests it's been a while since Slade has

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.969
been home, and the scene in the country store
suggests it's the re-emergence of the Titans

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:39.380
that has brought him back.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.600
When Slade and Wintergreen approach the arsenal,
for a moment it seems Slade may have forgotten

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:44.380
the passcode.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.780
Wintergreen offers to enter it himself, but
Slade insists.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.020
"It's my house."

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.340
The code he enters — "5639" — works, and
once you see the code you may wonder if it

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.020
was his memory that was bothering him, or
the code himself.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.680
The passcode isn't just a random grouping
of numbers.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.560
If you look closely at the letters corresponding
to the numbers on the keypad, it seems clear

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.440
they're based on a name: "Joey."

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:11.620
Joey is a meaningful name in the Deathstroke
mythos, as it's the first name of the superhero

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.860
Jericho, Slade Wilson's youngest son.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.200
Actor and activist Chella Man is set to play
him in upcoming episodes of Titans.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.980
The beginning of Season 2 not only sees the
Titans get a home, but gives us the appearance

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.340
of one of DC Comics' heroic monuments: Titans
Tower.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.080
In most media before now, Titans Tower has
usually been represented as it was originally

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.720
in the comics, as a giant T-shaped building.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.800
It's also usually separated from the mainland
on its own island.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.020
We don't get quite the same tower in Titans,
but the show does pay tribute to the comic

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.030
book version.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:45.799
Though the Tower clearly is made to look more
like a regular office building than the T-shaped

00:03:45.799 --> 00:03:49.819
headquarters of the comics, when the camera
pulls back from the Tower at the end of the

00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:53.599
season 2 premiere, the Titans' living space
is highlighted while a shadow falls on the

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.340
bottom half of the building, making it look
like a T.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.460
Probably the most blatant Easter egg we've
seen in Titans season 2 by the second episode

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.400
is a phone call Donna Troy gets after she
and Kori hand off the villain Shimmer to the

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.220
police.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:10.420
As her phone rings, she looks at the screen
and we see the name "Roy Harper" pop up.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:14.889
Roy Harper has had a number of different superhero
code names over the years, the most recent

00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:15.760
being Arsenal.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.419
He was once known as Speedy, Green Arrow's
sidekick.

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:23.070
Colton Haynes occasionally plays the character
on CW's Arrow, and in the comics he was one

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.660
of the many superheroes to die in the mini-series
Heroes in Crisis.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.740
Could this be a precursor to Arsenal joining
the Titans?

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.820
There's no news about such an addition, but
anything's possible.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.340
It could be that Donna and Roy are dating
and that's the reason for the phone call.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.840
Or perhaps Roy was a member of the previous
incarnation of the Titans, and — like everyone

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.660
else in the season 2's second episode — he
has news about the re-emergence of Doctor

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:45.280
Light.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.840
The third episode of Titans' second season,
"Ghosts," has a little bit of fun with DC

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.220
Comics' longtime competition.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:56.240
Hank, Dawn, and Donna finally show up to Titans
Tower to help hunt down Doctor Light, but

00:04:56.259 --> 00:04:59.419
they aren't too happy when Dick tells them
Deathstroke's daughter is chilling out in

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:00.660
a bedroom nearby.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.900
Shortly after the exchange, Dick visits Rose
in her bedroom and brings her a black eyepatch

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.040
to replace the white bandage she's been wearing
since she was introduced in the previous episode.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.680
Later that night, Rose has an uncharacteristically
friendly conversation with Rachel in the Titans'

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.500
kitchen.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.520
Rachel tells Rose her name is pretty.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.260
"Maybe too pretty now.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.820
'Patch' has a nice ring to it."

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.800
Likely, the show's creative team is having
a little fun at the expense of one of the

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.140
more, well, transparent alter egos in the
history of comics.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:32.060
Sure, Superman is silly for thinking he could
hide his secret identity with nothing but

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:36.340
a pair of glasses, but he's got nothing on
Marvel's Wolverine, who took on the least

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.360
convincing alter ego ever in the beginning
of his first ongoing solo series.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:44.620
While in Southeast Asia, Wolverine wore a
black eyepatch and navigated the criminal

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:49.610
underworld by calling himself "Patch," apparently
thinking no one would notice his trademark

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:50.200
hair.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.620
Nice try, Patch!

